    "reading": {
        "body": "Colorado passed rules for automated decision-making and conversational AI in 2026, but its two data center bills both failed.\n\nThe pattern in the 32 bills is that Colorado regulates what AI does to people and leaves alone what AI requires in hardware. SB189 on automated decision-making technology and HB1263 on conversational AI operator requirements both passed. HB1195 on psychotherapy AI and HB1139 on AI in health care were introduced. SB102 on large-load data centers and HB1030 on data center and utility modernization both failed, as did HB1246 on consumer-regulated electric utilities. The legislature acted on the outputs of models and not on the buildings and power that run them.\n\nThat split matters because compute is the binding constraint. A state can write operator requirements for chatbots without adding a single megawatt of supply, and Colorado did. The bills that would have touched large loads and utility modernization died, which leaves the physical side of AI scaling unaddressed in the one place where the state has direct authority over siting and rates.\n\nThe data does not say why the data center bills failed, how much load they covered, or whether the passed AI bills carry enforcement money. It also does not show federal action, utility commission proceedings, or private construction, so it cannot establish that Colorado has no data center policy at all. It covers one legislative session and three news items, one of which is about California.",
